  "timeMinutes": null,
  "type": "mc",
  "prompt": "A semi-circle is inscribed in an isosceles right-angled triangle as shown (legs of length 1). The radius of the semi-circle is",
  "image": "qimages/samf/senior-2004-r1/q20.webp",
  "choices": [
   {
    "key": "A",
    "text": "$\\frac1{\\sqrt2}$"
   },
   {
    "key": "B",
    "text": "$3-2\\sqrt2$"
   },
   {
    "key": "C",
    "text": "$\\frac12$"
   },
   {
    "key": "D",
    "text": "$2-\\sqrt2$"
   },
   {
    "key": "E",
    "text": "$\\sqrt2-1$"
   }
  ],
  "answer": "E",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Tangent lengths",
    "body": "Label the triangle $ABC$ with the right angle at $B$, so $AC=\\sqrt2$. Let $O$ be the centre of the semicircle (on $AB$) and $P$ the point of tangency on $AC$. Then $OP\\perp AC$ and $OP=OB=r$; the tangents from $C$ are equal: $PC=BC=1$. Triangle $APO$ is an isosceles right triangle, so $AP=OP=r$ and $AP+PC=AC$ gives $r+1=\\sqrt2$, $r=\\sqrt2-1$.\n\n**Answer: (E) $\\sqrt2-1$**"
   },
   {
    "title": "Coordinates",
    "body": "Place the right angle at the origin with the centre $(0,r)$ on the vertical leg. The hypotenuse is $x+y=1$, whose distance from the centre is $\\frac{1-r}{\\sqrt2}$. Setting this equal to $r$: $1-r=r\\sqrt2$, so $r=\\frac1{1+\\sqrt2}=\\sqrt2-1$.\n\n**Answer: (E) $\\sqrt2-1$**"
   }
  ],
  "topics": [
